## Why This Is Asked

Introduces a practical runtime plugin scenario: logging backends and formatters can be swapped without redeploying, exercising registry and strategy patterns in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern
- Strategy/Adapter via pluggable backends and formatters
- Open/Closed principle and runtime configurability

## Code Example

```typescript
interface LogBackend {
  log(level: string, message: string, meta?: any): void;
}
interface Formatter {
  format(level: string, message: string, meta?: any): string;
}
class Logger {
  private backends = new Map<string, LogBackend>();
  private formatter: Formatter;
  constructor(formatter: Formatter) { this.formatter = formatter; }
  register(name: string, backend: LogBackend) { this.backends.set(name, backend); }
  unregister(name: string) { this.backends.delete(name); }
  setFormatter(f: Formatter) { this.formatter = f; }
  log(level: string, message: string, meta?: any) {
    const formatted = this.formatter.format(level, message, meta);
    for (const b of this.backends.values()) b.log(level, formatted, meta);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle backpressure when multiple backends write synchronously?
- How would you ensure log order and handle failures in a backend without losing logs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of how to decouple variability in behavior using design patterns. It shows how to extend functionality without touching call sites, a common real-world need.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for interchangeable retry logic
- Dependency injection to supply policies
- Clear separation of fetch logic from retry strategy
- Testability by swapping mock policies

## Code Example
```javascript
interface RetryPolicy {
  shouldRetry(attempt: number, err: any): boolean;
  nextDelay(attempt: number, err?: any): number;
}
class LinearPolicy implements RetryPolicy {
  constructor(private delay: number, private max: number) {}
  shouldRetry(attempt: number) { return attempt < this.max; }
  nextDelay(_attempt: number) { return this.delay; }
}
class ExponentialPolicy implements RetryPolicy {
  constructor(private base: number, private factor: number, private cap: number) {}
  shouldRetry(attempt: number) { return attempt * this.factor < this.cap; }
  nextDelay(attempt: number) { return Math.min(this.base * Math.pow(this.factor, attempt - 1), this.cap); }
}
```

```javascript
const policy = new ExponentialPolicy(100, 2, 1000);
fetchUser('alice', policy);
```

